
<p>Matt Birchler:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>So, yeah, I do think that, especially this year, unit sales will
far lag behind the 18 Pro models, the normal iPhone 18, and
probably the Air 2 and 18e. I think they will lag next year as
well for similar reasons. I don’t know if this will ever become
the <em>most</em> popular iPhone, but I do not think it will fail, and I
think it will sell more units every year for its first few years
on the market (especially if they can get the price down over that
time as well).</p>

<p>My evidence for this is the increasing market share folding phones
have in the Android world, as well as my personal experience using
something very similar to what Apple is about to announce and
having my own aha moment, where it really clicked with me.</p>
</blockquote>

<p>Birchler has been using a Samsung Galaxy Fold8 — the new one that preemptively copied the folding iPhone’s aspect ratio — for the last month or so. So I’m intrigued by his firsthand experience with the same general form factor. But I have no idea what he’s talking about regarding “the increasing market share folding phones have in the Android world”. For 2025, <a href="https://counterpointresearch.com/en/insights/Foldable-Smartphone-Market-Set-for-20-percent-Growth-in-2026">Counterpoint</a> and <a href="https://www.trendforce.com/presscenter/news/20250722-12649.html">TrendForce</a> both pegged foldable phones at 1.6% of the global market. Just <em>today</em> <a href="https://counterpointresearch.com/en/insights/global-foldable-shipments-to-hit-100-million-cumulative-milestone-by-end-of-2026">Counterpoint released a report</a> that says “Foldables still account for only about 2% of the overall smartphone market, leaving plenty of room for the category to grow.”</p>

<p>Anecdotally I find it hard to believe the 2 percent number. It’s my nature to take notice of the phones people use, and there’s no way that 1 in 50 people I see have foldable phones. I don’t even think it’s 1 in 100. On a recent vacation, I didn’t see a single foldable at the airport nor at the resort hotel where we stayed. Obviously my own anecdata could be all wet, but I think “2 percent” is generous — and presumably, if it’s accurate, it’s almost entirely from outside the U.S. It’s like saying “touchscreen phones” had “increasing market share” before the iPhone. Going from 0.1% to 0.2% is a doubling of share, but the share is still tiny.</p>

<p>Regarding the name, Birchler spitballs a decent suggestion:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>Finally, there’s the name. Everyone seems to think it’s going to
be the iPhone Ultra, and I guess if I were putting money down,
that’s what I’d bet on as well.</p>

<p>But I just don’t like it. I don’t like it because it’s not
“ultra” in many ways, and I don’t like it because what do you
call the bigger version of it in a year or two? The Ultra Max?
Maybe you do…</p>

<p>I personally think it should be called the iPhone Folio. I think
there’s a 99% chance I’m wrong, but hey, won’t it be amazing if
I’m right?</p>
</blockquote>

<p>The problem with “Folio” is that it’s too descriptive only of the form factor. Not the feeling it’s supposed to convey. I’m betting on “Ultra”, but I’m <a href="https://daringfireball.net/linked/2026/04/14/name-of-foldable-iphone">still rooting for “Duo”</a>, which <a href="https://everymac.com/systems/apple/powerbook_duo/index-powerbook-duo.html">has some history</a> <em>and</em> sounds like a nice companion adjective to “Neo”.</p>

